title: Cloud Security Best Practices
page: cloud-security-best-practices.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: Master the first three practice areas (identity, configuration and network) and you will prevent the majority of cloud incidents seen in industry.

== Ranked by what breaks in real incidents
layout: text
section: top
Most "cloud security best practices" lists are long and unranked. This one is ordered by what appears as the root cause in our breach kill chains. The later areas are where mature programs differentiate.
notes:
Most cloud security best practices lists are 100 items long and unranked, which the page says is the same as not having a list. The areas here are ordered by what appears as the root cause in the site's breach kill chains.

The page says that if you master the first three sections, you will prevent the majority of cloud incidents seen in industry. The later sections are where mature programs differentiate.

If a practice is not here, the page says that is because it calls out the ones with the biggest payoff, not because the practice does not matter.

== The eight practice areas
layout: compare
section: practice-overview
col: First four | Identity and access | Configuration and posture | Network controls | Data protection and secrets
col: Last four | Logging and detection | Supply chain and CI/CD | Workloads and containers | AI and LLM workloads
Ranked roughly by how often the absence of each shows up in real cloud incidents.
notes:
The eight areas are ranked roughly by how often the absence of each shows up in real cloud incidents. Identity and access is the most important: MFA, least privilege and no long-lived keys.

Configuration and posture covers CIS benchmarks, public-block-by-default and encryption-by-default. Network controls cover segmentation, private endpoints and egress filtering. Data protection covers KMS, secrets managers, classification and exfiltration prevention.

Then logging and detection, supply chain and CI/CD, workloads and containers, and finally AI and LLM workloads.

== A maturity ladder
layout: figure
section: practice-overview
figure: csm-title
Start with the Stage 2 essentials, then layer Stage 3 tooling.
notes:
The figure shows a cloud security maturity model with four stages, from reactive to optimized, each with characteristic practices and tooling.

The page says the practices that follow are roughly ordered by where they show up in this ladder. Start with the Stage 2 essentials, and layer Stage 3 tooling once those are in place.

== Fix identity first
layout: text
section: identity
If you only fix one category, fix identity. Most cloud breaches we have documented involve an identity failure somewhere in the chain. At Capital One, a WAF role had S3 list and read across 700 buckets.
notes:
The page says that if you only fix one category, fix this one, because identity is the perimeter. Most cloud breaches the site has documented involve an identity failure somewhere in the chain.

The Capital One breach happened because a WAF role had S3 list and read across 700 buckets. The page says read-only on one bucket would have stopped it.

== Identity checklist
layout: checklist
section: identity
check: Phishing-resistant MFA on every human identity
check: Least privilege on every workload identity
check: Eliminate long-lived access keys
check: Enforce IMDSv2 on every EC2 instance
check: Just-in-time access for privileged operations
check: One IdP for everything
check: Audit Conditional Access and IAM policies regularly
notes:
Push prompts and SMS codes are bypassable, and the page points to Uber 2022 and Scattered Spider at MGM. FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and platform authenticators are the bar, enforced through Conditional Access or IAM policies and not just policy documents.

For least privilege, the page names IAM Access Analyzer on AWS, Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management and Google's IAM Recommender for finding and trimming unused permissions continuously.

Use short-lived federated credentials through OIDC for CI/CD, workload-identity federation for service-to-service, and SSO for humans. IMDSv2 closes the Capital One pattern of SSRF to IMDSv1 to temporary credentials to S3 exfiltration. AWS makes v2 the default only on some launch paths, so set the account-level default and enforce it org-wide with an SCP.

Standing admin is standing risk, so use just-in-time elevation with approval and time bounds. Federate everything to one IdP so joiner, mover and leaver events fire in one place. Policies drift, so weekly automated reports close gaps before attackers find them.

== How identity nests
layout: figure
section: identity
figure: ih-title
SCP or org-policy at the root is the cheapest control by orders of magnitude.
notes:
The figure is a top-down view of how cloud identity nests: organization at the top, then accounts, then roles and service principals, then identities, then permissions.

The page's caption says the deeper a permission lives, the more painful misconfiguration is at the top, and that an SCP or org-policy at the root is the cheapest control by orders of magnitude.

== Configuration and posture
layout: checklist
section: configuration
check: Continuous posture scanning across all accounts
check: Default-deny on public access
check: Encryption-at-rest defaults, enforced by policy
check: Tag for ownership and blast radius
check: Guardrail accounts, not exception lists
check: Drift-detect Infrastructure as Code
notes:
The page says misconfigurations are a common breach root cause, but that posture management tools have made this category solvable for most organizations.

Run a CSPM tool, with Prowler and ScoutSuite as the open-source defaults, scan against CIS benchmarks or your provider's equivalent, and fix the findings instead of just collecting them. Revoke default-public IAM grants on day one of every new account.

Enforce encryption through SCP, Azure Policy or Org Policy so users cannot disable it. Give each business unit its own account, subscription or project, with floor-level controls enforced by policy and cross-account access made explicit. Run terraform plan in CI on every change, and use AWS Config, Azure Policy or GCP Asset Inventory to catch console-mode quick fixes.

== Network controls
layout: table
section: network
row: Control | What it does
row: Default-deny security groups | No 0.0.0.0/0 on port 22 or 3389 except deliberate jump hosts
row: Private endpoints | Keep managed-service traffic off the public internet
row: Egress and DNS filtering | Catch HTTPS exfiltration once log monitoring is wired up
row: Zero trust between services | mTLS or workload-identity authorization
row: WAF with modern rules | SSRF and OWASP Top 10 rules; defaults are not enough
Cloud networks are not perimeters in the on-prem sense, but network controls still matter, especially for limiting lateral movement and exfiltration.
notes:
Cloud networks are not perimeters in the on-prem sense, but the controls still matter, especially for limiting lateral movement and exfiltration.

Private Link and VPC endpoints reduce attack surface and give you flow logs you can audit. The page says most data exfiltration goes out over HTTPS to attacker-controlled domains, and that egress proxies, NAT-gateway logs and DNS filtering catch it when log monitoring is wired up.

For zero trust between services, use mTLS through a service mesh or workload-identity-based authorization, and assume an attacker is already on the LAN. For WAFs, the page says defaults are not enough: add explicit rules for SSRF, blocking requests to 169.254.169.254, and for the OWASP Top 10. It also covers DDoS protection on internet-facing services.

== Data protection and secrets
layout: checklist
section: data
check: Classify sensitive data and let classes drive policy
check: Customer-managed keys for sensitive data
check: Secrets in a managed store, never in code
check: Encrypt in transit, end to end
check: Back up, and test the restore
check: DLP on data egress paths
notes:
Microsoft Purview, AWS Macie and Google DLP can find PII, PCI and PHI in storage, and the classifications drive access policy, encryption requirements and incident-response priority. Customer-managed keys give you a rotation policy, deletion control and a stop-the-bleeding lever during an incident.

Use a managed secrets store, auto-rotate where supported, and run gitleaks, trufflehog or GitHub secret scanning in CI. For transit, the page says TLS 1.2 minimum and 1.3 preferred, and treats the trust store as part of the control: a bundled CA list in a container or device image commits you to every root in it, revocation often lags a compromised certificate, and short-lived certificates are what keep that window small.

Untested backups do not count. Pair DLP with egress controls so the alert fires before the data leaves.

== The detection baseline
layout: figure
section: data | detection
figure: detection-baseline
Logging and detection, reduced to the order that produces a detection program.
notes:
The figure shows four steps from the logging section: turn on native detection, centralize control-plane logs, write detections as code, then test them and rehearse.

The page's caption calls this the logging section reduced to the order that produces a detection program.

== Logging and detection
layout: checklist
section: detection
check: Enable native threat detection in every account
check: Centralize control-plane logs
check: Build detection content as code
check: Alert on impossible behavior, not signatures
check: Test your detections
check: Rehearse incident response
notes:
You cannot respond to what you do not see, and the page says the SolarWinds, Capital One and LastPass incidents all had detectable activity that nothing was watching for.

GuardDuty, Defender for Cloud and Security Command Center are the cheapest credible detection layer available, so turn them on org-wide. Send CloudTrail, Activity Log and Audit Logs to a data lake or SIEM, with retention of 90+ days for incident response and longer for compliance.

Keep Sigma, KQL or SPL detections versioned and peer-reviewed, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK Cloud techniques. Behavior-based detections survive attacker tooling changes. Detection content that has not been exercised in production conditions probably does not fire. Run tabletop exercises quarterly and real exercises at least annually.

== Supply chain and CI/CD
layout: checklist
section: supply-chain
check: Pin dependencies to exact versions
check: SBOM for every artifact; sign with Sigstore/cosign
check: Hardened, SLSA-style build pipelines
check: OIDC, not stored credentials, for cloud deploys
check: Scope CI tokens minimally
check: Pin GitHub Actions to a full SHA
notes:
The page says modern attackers pivot through your build pipeline, with SolarWinds as the canonical case and npm and PyPI typosquats and the Codecov compromise as routine.

Commit lockfiles, and use Renovate or Dependabot for bumps with PR review. Produce SBOMs in SPDX or CycloneDX format, and sign with Sigstore/cosign so you can prove a binary came from your build. Builds should run in isolated, hermetic environments with signed provenance attestations.

OIDC from GitHub Actions to AWS, Azure or GCP eliminates long-lived deploy keys, so there is no secret to leak. A test-runner job does not need write access to production. Tags can be moved silently by a compromised maintainer, while SHAs cannot.

== Workloads and containers
layout: table
section: workloads
row: Practice | Detail
row: Scan images | At build and in the registry; a clean image can rot
row: Run non-root | Read-only root filesystem, dropped capabilities, seccomp
row: Network Policies | Default-deny inter-pod traffic
row: Runtime detection | Falco or Tetragon catch process-level anomalies
row: Patch both planes | You still own node images and worker patching
row: No public K8s API | Private endpoints, bastion and audit logging
Block builds on critical CVEs in base layers.
notes:
Scan container images with a tool such as Trivy or Grype at build and again in the registry. The page says to block builds on critical CVEs in base layers, and that a clean image at build can rot.

Run as non-root with a read-only root filesystem, with Pod Security Standards restricted as the baseline on Kubernetes. Without Network Policies, a compromised pod is on a flat network. Falco and Tetragon are eBPF-based and catch things like an unexpected shell or privilege escalation.

Managed Kubernetes handles control-plane patching, but you still own node-image refreshes and worker patching. Do not expose Kubernetes APIs directly to the internet; the page calls the kubelet API a known attack target.

== AI and LLM workloads
layout: checklist
section: ai
check: Treat every LLM input as untrusted
check: Limit agent tool authority
check: Sandbox indirect prompt injection sources
check: Log model inputs, outputs and tool calls
check: Review sensitive data sent to external model APIs
check: Test against the OWASP LLM Top 10
notes:
The page calls this the newest category and the one with the most movement, and says to treat AI workloads as agentic systems with tool access.

Prompt injection is the default vulnerability of any system that lets a model see attacker-controllable text. Guardrails help but are not sufficient. If an agent can call APIs, scope those calls to least privilege with the same discipline as IAM, because confused-deputy attacks are real.

Retrieved web pages, emails and PDFs are attacker-controlled, so strip HTML, summarize through a constrained sub-call, and never feed raw retrieved content into a tool-calling context. Log model inputs, outputs and tool calls, tagged with user identity. Send production traffic to external model APIs through DLP and use enterprise tier endpoints with no-training guarantees for sensitive workloads.

== Zero trust at every layer
layout: figure
section: ai
figure: zt-title
Verify explicitly, use least privilege, assume breach.
notes:
The figure shows the three foundational principles of zero trust: verify explicitly, use least privilege access, and assume breach.

The page's caption says to apply all three at every layer of the defense-in-depth stack: the perimeter, the network, identity, workloads and data.

== Governance and people
layout: checklist
section: governance
check: Document the shared responsibility line per service
check: Run cloud security through your SDLC
check: Train developers and admins continuously
check: Rehearse your incident response plan
check: Vendor risk for the vendors of your vendors
notes:
The page says most cloud security failures are misunderstandings of who is supposed to do what, so document the shared responsibility line for every service you use.

Threat model at design, scan IaC at the PR, scan posture at deploy and monitor runtime in production. A separate security org becomes a bottleneck nobody calls. The page calls annual compliance training theater, and suggests hands-on labs, tabletop exercises and breach walkthroughs.

An incident response plan covers roles, escalation paths, communication templates and legal and PR triggers. For vendors, SaaS your SaaS uses can leak your data without you noticing, so ask for SOC 2 reports of subprocessors as well as direct vendors.

== Build the controls once
layout: figure
section: governance
figure: cv-title
Most cloud configuration evidence maps to SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS.
notes:
The figure is a three-circle Venn diagram showing how SOC 2, ISO 27001 and PCI DSS share a common control core, with framework-specific extensions on the outside.

The page's caption says most cloud configuration evidence, such as CloudTrail, IAM policies and encryption settings, maps to all three frameworks. Build the controls once, and tag them per framework for the auditor.

== Anti-patterns: things to stop doing
layout: list
section: anti-patterns
If you see any of these in your environment, fix them before working on more sophisticated controls. The eighth: "We'll add monitoring later."
item: Long-lived IAM access keys for service-to-service auth
item: Public S3 buckets for convenience
item: Wildcard IAM policies
item: SSH into instances to debug
item: "Just give me admin and I'll figure out what I need later"
item: Using the root account for daily work
item: Email as MFA
notes:
The page frames these as the flip side of the best practices: if you see any of them in your environment, fix them before working on more sophisticated controls.

Use OIDC federation or workload identity instead of long-lived keys. Use signed URLs, CloudFront with OAC or proper authentication instead of public buckets. Scope even admin roles to the actions and resources that are needed. Use Session Manager, Azure Bastion or Google IAP, which leave audit trails and do not require open inbound 22.

For the admin request, the right answer is to find out what is needed and grant exactly that, and IAM Access Advisor or Access Analyzer can answer that after a trial period. Lock the root credentials in a vault with hardware MFA and break glass only for billing or account closure. Email is push-bombable, phishable and account-takeover-able, so use FIDO2 or platform authenticators.

The eighth anti-pattern is "we'll add monitoring later." Moving production workloads to the cloud without GuardDuty, Defender or Security Command Center enabled means flying blind from day one, so turn it on before you turn the workload on.

== Why is identity first, not last?
layout: question
section: identity
Think about what most of the documented breach chains have in common.
notes:
Ask this and let the room answer before moving on.

== Identity is the new perimeter
layout: answer
section: identity
In the cloud, identity is the new perimeter, and most cloud breaches involve at least one identity failure. That is why identity comes first.
notes:
The page's answer is that in the cloud, identity is the new perimeter, and most cloud breaches involve at least one identity failure. That is why the identity section is first, not last.

== Why test your detections?
layout: question
section: detection
Think about a rule that has never been exercised.
notes:
Ask this and let the room answer before moving on. The page names Atomic Red Team and Stratus Red Team as examples of tools for this.

== Untested rules probably do not fire
layout: answer
section: detection
Detection content that has not been exercised in production conditions probably does not fire. Test it, run tabletop exercises quarterly and real exercises at least annually.
notes:
The page says detection content that has not been exercised in production conditions is detection content that probably does not fire. Test with tools such as Atomic Red Team, Stratus Red Team, the GuardDuty Tester or Microsoft Attack Simulator.

Run tabletop exercises quarterly, and real exercises, with the SOC team responding in a sandbox account, at least annually. Rehearse the embarrassing scenarios.
